1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,626
We wanted to understand how generic and brand-name medicines contribute to patients’ out-of-pocket costs,

2
00:00:05,626 --> 00:00:11,496
so we analyzed a nationally representative survey of prescription use and spending in twenty twenty-two.

3
00:00:11,496 --> 00:00:17,833
More than half of Americans filled a generic prescription, compared with about one in six who filled a brand-name prescription.

4
00:00:17,833 --> 00:00:20,721
Although brand-name drugs were more expensive per medicine,

5
00:00:20,721 --> 00:00:25,701
generics accounted for fifty-five percent of total out-of-pocket prescription spending.

6
00:00:25,701 --> 00:00:30,347
Our findings suggest that policies to reduce patients’ costs should address both.

7
00:00:30,347 --> 00:00:35,012
Lower brand-name prices could reduce what patients pay through deductibles and coinsurance.

8
00:00:35,012 --> 00:00:38,150
For generics, we highlight changes in insurance benefits:

9
00:00:38,150 --> 00:00:41,426
limiting out-of-pocket costs for high-value generic medicines,

10
00:00:41,426 --> 00:00:44,221
preventing patient charges from exceeding cash prices,

11
00:00:44,221 --> 00:00:47,070
or eliminating generic copayments altogether.

12
00:00:47,070 --> 00:00:52,652
These approaches address both the prices of medicines and how insurance determines patients’ costs.

13
00:00:52,652 --> 00:00:55,327
You can read our full study in JAMA Network Open.
